in my introductory video about taoism  i talked about how the religion has  taken on many  different forms throughout history and  that there's no  use in calling it a coherent religion in  the regular sense  everything from a politically involved  and strongly ritualistic practice to  the total escape from society and  free-flowing ascetic  attitudes it all fits under the wider  umbrella of taoism and the ways that it  has been approached  one example of the later kind we find in  an early example of taoist practice and  religion  with a group of scholars who have come  to have a legendary status in the  chinese cultural consciousness  and who are collectively known as the  seven sages  of the bamboo grove  the third century was not a very  pleasant time for china  it saw the collapse of the long-standing  han dynasty  in 220 and the subsequent descent into  political chaos  for most of the century rivaling  kingdoms and power structures  fought and ward amongst each other in  what has become known  as the infamous three kingdoms period  immortalized in different books  movies and video games and in this  period of great unrest the political and  social world was  filled with violence with corruption and  jealousy  as if that isn't the case otherwise but  while this may paint a very bleak  picture  such times of unrest can also sometimes  lead to flourishing or  very interesting developments in other  areas of society  and in a way such was the case here for  as a result of this environment we get a  very  fascinating and incredibly chilled out  group of scholars who would  as i said come to have a legendary  status to this day  you see there were a group of seven  scholars poets and  musicians who all were of pretty high  status and prominence in way dynasty  china  but they became so disillusioned with  the political corruption of  politics and the government that they  would escape to the countryside into a  specific  bamboo grove surrounded by beautiful  nature  here they would gather together to  appreciate the beauty of nature and to  compose  poetry play music together and drink  wine  some stories even claim that they may  have consumed some psychedelic  substances  now all of this was a way to escape the  chaotic and dangerous  world in the so-called civilized society  they could all be seen as taoists as  this retreat was  quintessentially a kind of taoist  practice  one of the more organized forms of  taoism that had emerged at this time was  a movement known as  jintan or pure conversation  it was characterized by an advocating of  free living  individual expression and a quote  hedonistic escape from the corrupt court  politics of the short-lived wei dynasty  and the seven sages of the bamboo grove  can very much be said to belong to this  school not as chintan or pure  conversation  if you remember the main principles of  taoism it talks about  wu way or effortless action to live  freely spontaneously and in harmony with  nature  and the tao the seven sages very much  represent this  a rejection of a kind of confusion  legalism by escaping into nature and  appreciating it  as well as flowing or being one with the  tao  through composing poetry music  and having pure conversations free  discussions among friends about  metaphysical and other philosophical  questions  i would totally like to hang out with  these people these seven sages of the  bamboo grove included  ji kang a writer poet musician and  alchemist whose  estate the bamboo group was located next  to  he was apparently an accomplished player  of the gucci  back then known only as qin which is a  chinese string instrument that has a  strong connection with taoism in  particular  there was also luijin a taoist poet and  apparently a  great lover of alcohol there was ruan  chan  another musician and player of the  ancient chinese loot  and the modern development of that  instrument that one is actually named  after him  there was chan zhou a great taoist  scholar who wrote famous commentaries on  the juanse one of the foundational texts  of taoism  wang rong an official and scholar of the  jin dynasty  there was chan tao which was another  musician and as his name suggests a  prominent taoist  and lastly the sage that is probably the  most renowned of the seven  ruan ji who was a master musician and  poet who also played the instrument  gutshin  he used to write poetry that strongly  criticized and spoke out against the  ruling class but did  so through allegory so as to hide it  from those he actually criticized  he is also said to have purposefully  remained drunk for 60 days just to avoid  being forced into an arranged political  marriage  also he seems to have a significant  impact on music and wrote many  compositions  one of the melodies that is attributed  to him known as drunken ecstasy or  drunken madness  still survives and can be heard on many  gucci  recordings these sages all had the  common wish to  escape from the world and instead live  in simplicity  in the bamboo grove they could express  their taoist ideals and love of nature  through their music and poetry some  stories for example tell how they would  enter ecstatic states jump into the  water to cool off and then start writing  inspired verses of  poetry the story of the seven sages of  the bamboo grove is a  legendary one it is of course incredibly  romantic and  inspiring in a way i think many of us  can relate to the sages and their wish  to escape  a chaotic society the question of the  authenticity of this story is undecided  the  seven sages themselves were indeed real  people who lived at this time but some  argue that the story of their regular  gathering in this bamboo grove may be  just that a story but irregardless of  its historical authenticity  the seven sages have come to play a  legendary role in the collective  consciousness of the chinese people  and to some degree outside china as well  they represent a wish for a simpler life  one  free from the complex world of politics  and societal upheaval  a life that is free expressive in the  company of good companions and in tune  and appreciation of the beauty of nature  and our connection to it the seven sages  are thus also a perfect expression of  some of the most central ideas  in the religion of taoism i think we can  all recognize ourselves  in the sages in these current times of  pandemics wars political instability and  polarization  the idea of just escaping it all to  enjoy some nice music  poetry and nature together with your  friends is  kind of unnervingly tempting so if i  suddenly stop publishing videos for a  while  you know where to find me i'll see you  next time  [Music]  you
 

